{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfac7RC2cTNdbYoxeiPXy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The setting is implemented in `lib/utils.js` and `lib/application.js`.\n\n- **Normalizing to a predicate:** `compileTrust` in `lib/utils.js:194` turns the `trust proxy` value into a function. `app.set` in `lib/application.js:370` calls it and stores the result as the `trust proxy fn` setting.\n- **Sub-app inheritance:** the mount handler in `app.init` (`lib/application.js:101-114`) deletes the sub-app's own `trust proxy` and `trust proxy fn` settings when the parent has a `trust proxy fn` function. The sub-app then falls back to the parent's value. The \"inherit back-compat\" comments around lines 101 and 373 mark this logic. The back-compat logic sets a default and then overrides it only if the sub-app never set its own value. I did not read that code closely, so I'm not certain of that part.\n- **Where the predicate is used:** `lib/request.js` reads `trust proxy fn` in the getters at lines 288, 328, 345 and 406.\n\n```json\n{\"files\": [\"lib/application.js\", \"lib/utils.js\", \"lib/request.js\"], \"symbols\": [{\"path\": \"lib/utils.js\", \"name\": \"exports.compileTrust\"}, {\"path\": \"lib/application.js\", \"name\": \"app.set\"}, {\"path\": \"lib/application.js\", \"name\": \"app.defaultConfiguration\"}, {\"path\": \"lib/application.js\", \"name\": \"app.init\"}]}\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":708,"cache_read_input_tokens":25265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":708},"output_tokens":492,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5005dbd3-a20c-5bc1-a9de-03446f6d75b8","uuid":"8a684e95-d1c2-4b90-ba0a-68f1d3116be8","timestam